        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "SSRF via unvalidated HTTP redirect following in `_http_get` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:250-267). On a 302 response the function recursively calls itself with the fully server-controlled `Location` response header: `if response.status == 302: return _http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])` (line 262). The recursive invocation then does `conn = http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc)` (line 256) and `conn.request(\"GET\", path_and_query)` (line 258) against that attacker-supplied netloc, with NO host/IP allowlist, no scheme validation, no private/link-local address filter, and no redirect-count cap. `_http_get` is reached from two entry points: `scan_url(url)` (line 628: `scan_bytes(io.BytesIO(_http_get(url)), url)`) and `scan_huggingface_model(repo_id)` (line 578 and line 589: `io.BytesIO(_http_get(url))`), both wired to CLI flags `-u/--url` and `-hf/--huggingface` (cli.py:66, cli.py:68). Any server the scanner is pointed at -- or any HuggingFace-hosted model repo, since HF legitimately 302-redirects LFS files to a CDN, which is why this redirect support exists -- can redirect picklescan to an arbitrary internal host, e.g. `Location: https://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` or an internal admin service. Note also that the parsed scheme is discarded: `HTTPSConnection` is used unconditionally on whatever netloc the redirect supplies. The fetched body is then parsed/scanned and partially reflected into logs and the `ScanResult`, giving a response-content oracle.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. _http_get follows HTTP 302 redirects by recursing on the fully server-controlled Location response header (scanner.py:261-262), and the recursive call opens a connection to that attacker-supplied netloc (scanner.py:256 HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc), 258 conn.request). I grepped the entire repository for 302/redirect/allowlist/netloc/HTTPSConnection: there is exactly one redirect handler and NO host/IP allowlist, NO private/link-local filter, NO scheme check and NO max-redirect cap anywhere in src/ or tests/ \u2014 so the analyst's \"no guard\" claim is true, not merely unproven. Reachability holds in both source and graph: lines 256/258/262 are reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, and EntryPoints _http_get(250)/scan_url(625)/scan_huggingface_model(574) are all exposure:reachable; source-verified call sites scanner.py:628, 578, 589 are driven by CLI flags -u/--url (cli.py:66) and -hf/--huggingface (cli.py:68). A scanned server (exactly the untrusted input this tool exists to consume) can thus redirect the scanner to an arbitrary internal host:port, yielding forced internal GETs, an internal host/port existence oracle via distinguishable failures and the status/reason reflected in the RuntimeError at 264-266, and body disclosure where TLS validates; an absent cap also allows unbounded self-recursion to RecursionError (secondary CWE-674). I correct the analyst on three points and downgrade HIGH to MEDIUM: (1) the cited PoC https://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ does not work, because line 256 forces HTTPSConnection and Python's default ssl._create_default_https_context() verifies cert AND hostname (PEP 476) while cloud IMDS is plaintext HTTP/80 \u2014 so content exfiltration requires the internal endpoint to present a cert valid for the redirect hostname; (2) discarding parsed_url.scheme is real but CONSTRAINS the attacker to HTTPS targets rather than enabling http://file://; (3) the HuggingFace vector is mis-described, since repo_id is interpolated only after the netloc (578, 588) and cannot itself select a redirect target. Version pinned at picklescan 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3) with empty install_requires, so no dependency-CVE angle; exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", giving no published-exploit signal either way, so this verdict rests only on source and graph evidence I gathered."
          },
