        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-674",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unbounded recursion / stack-exhaustion DoS in `_http_get` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:250-267). The 302 handler at line 262 recurses via `return _http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])` with no depth counter, no visited-URL set, and no maximum-redirect parameter -- the function's only parameter is `url`. A malicious or compromised server (or a redirect loop reached through `scan_url`/`scan_huggingface_model`) that answers every request with `302 Location: <self>` drives infinite mutual recursion until Python's recursion limit is hit, raising RecursionError; each frame also holds an un-closed `HTTPSConnection`, so sockets/file descriptors accumulate across the ~1000 frames before unwinding. I verified by query that no `max_redirect`-style guard exists anywhere under src/.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source independently. `_http_get` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:250) accepts only `url` -- no depth, limit, or visited-set parameter -- and line 262 unconditionally self-recurses on any HTTP 302: `return _http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])`. I read the entire function body (250-269) and the only two conditionals are `response.status == 302` (261) and `response.status >= 400` (263); there is no counter increment, depth comparison, or loop detection. My own grep for `max_redirect|sys.setrecursionlimit` across the whole tree returned zero hits, so no cap exists elsewhere either. A server answering every request with `302 Location: <self>` therefore recurses until Python's recursion limit raises RecursionError. The claim is reachable from a real entry point, confirmed in source rather than only in the graph: cli.py:34 registers `-u/--url`, cli.py:66 calls `scan_url(args.url)` and cli.py:68 calls `scan_huggingface_model(...)`, which reach `_http_get` at scanner.py:628, 578 and 589; the graph independently marks all four src call sites (including the 262 self-call) `reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1`. The analyst's socket claim is substantially correct: `conn.close()` sits in a `finally` (268-269) that cannot execute until the recursive call returns, so at depth N there are N simultaneously-live HTTPSConnections -- with the default ~1000 limit and a typical `ulimit -n` of 1024, FD exhaustion is plausible before RecursionError. I correct that it is a transient peak during unwind, not a permanent leak as \"un-closed\" implies. I also downgrade the analyst's HIGH severity: cli.py:77-79 catches `except Exception`, and RecursionError subclasses RuntimeError subclasses Exception, so on the primary CLI path this is a logged exception and `return 2`, not a crash. Genuine impact is limited to library embedders of the public `scan_url`/`scan_huggingface_model` API, where RecursionError propagates and the socket peak can starve a long-lived service -- a self-inflicted DoS triggered by a server the operator chose to contact. Hence LOW, not HIGH. Advisory exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so it provided no signal in either direction; this is a first-party code defect, not a versioned-dependency question, so no version pin applies. Separately out of scope: the `Location` header is used unvalidated to build `HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc)`, an independent SSRF/open-redirect concern (CWE-601/918)."
          },
