        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Path traversal / Zip-Slip in `scan_7z_bytes` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:428-447). Archive member names are taken straight from the untrusted 7z file -- `file_names = archive.getnames()` (line 438) -- and the ONLY filter applied is a suffix test, `targets = [f for f in file_names if f.endswith(tuple(_pickle_file_extensions))]` (line 439). That attacker-controlled `targets` list is then passed to `archive.extract(path=tmpdir, targets=targets)` (line 442), which writes to disk, and each name is re-joined as `file_path = os.path.join(tmpdir, file_name)` (line 444) before `os.path.isfile(file_path)` (446) and `scan_file_path(file_path)` (447). There is no containment validation of any kind between the archive and the extract/join -- no normpath, no realpath, no commonpath, and no `startswith(tmpdir)` check; I confirmed by query that the only path-normalization call in the whole of src/ is an unrelated `os.path.abspath(args.path)` in cli.py:58. A 7z entry named e.g. `../../../../home/victim/.bashrc.pkl` or `..\\..\\..\\startup\\x.pkl` satisfies the `.pkl` suffix filter and escapes the TemporaryDirectory on extraction, yielding arbitrary file write (and, via os.path.join, scanning/leaking of files outside tmpdir into the ScanResult). Exploitable through `scan_file_path`/`scan_directory_path` on any attacker-supplied `.7z`, and reached automatically from `scan_pytorch` line 525 (`_is_7z_file(data)` -> `scan_7z_bytes(data, file_id)`). Severity depends partly on whether the installed py7zr version sanitizes member names, but picklescan itself adds no defense.\n\nVerifier (INCONCLUSIVE): The code defect the lead describes is REAL and present verbatim where claimed: scanner.py:437-449 takes archive member names from an untrusted 7z via archive.getnames() (438), filters them ONLY by suffix (439), and passes that attacker-controlled list straight to archive.extract(path=tmpdir, targets=targets) (442) and os.path.join(tmpdir, file_name) (444) with zero containment validation. I independently grepped all of src/ for normpath/realpath/abspath/commonpath/commonprefix/relpath/is_relative_to/resolve/startswith and the only path-normalization in the package is the unrelated os.path.abspath(args.path) at cli.py:58 - so the analyst's \"no defense in picklescan\" claim is confirmed by my own evidence. Reachability is also confirmed from source, not just graph labels: scan_bytes:568-569 routes to scan_7z_bytes on MAGIC BYTES alone (_is_7z_file), independent of file extension, and is reached from scan_file_path:622, scan_directory_path:612, scan_url:628 and scan_huggingface_model:590, all four exposed by cli.py:62-68; the graph agrees (every call at 437-447 has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, and scan_7z_bytes is an :EntryPoint kind=handler exposure=reachable). What I could NOT establish is the lead's claimed IMPACT (arbitrary file write / Zip-Slip), because the write primitive lives in py7zr, not picklescan. py7zr is pinned at ==0.22.0 (requirements.txt:9 and :Dependency), which is PAST the 0.20.2 fix for CVE-2022-44900 (directory traversal via ../ in 7z member names, affecting py7zr 0.20.0 and earlier), so extract() most likely rejects or sanitizes traversal names and the arbitrary-write claim is probably neutralized by the library rather than by picklescan. I could not verify py7zr 0.22.0's actual behavior (raise vs. strip) in this session: py7zr is not installed anywhere on this filesystem (globs over /usr/lib/python3, /usr/local/lib, /home/lutherleo, /home/lutherleo/.cache found no py7zr) and I have no shell, and exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed (run `orion index-exploits`)\" so I could not cite a module/CVE version range. If 0.22.0 raises Bad7zFile, the only consequence is an exception (DoS at most); if it strips the leading ../, no write escapes but picklescan's own unsanitized os.path.join at 444 would still stat and scan_file_path a path OUTSIDE tmpdir, giving a limited read / existence oracle whose output is restricted to pickle global names. Per the rules I will not guess between those outcomes. Separately, I did verify a related real weakness the lead did not mention: setup.cfg:25 declares the extra as \"7z=py7zr\" with NO minimum version, so a downstream `pip install picklescan[7z]` in an environment holding py7zr <= 0.20.0 would be exposed to the CVE-2022-44900 traversal through this exact unvalidated code path - a dependency-specification gap, not proof of the claimed traversal. Verdict: missing zip-slip containment is a confirmed defense-in-depth defect at the cited location; exploitability at the pinned py7zr version is UNKNOWN, so INCONCLUSIVE rather than CONFIRM."
          },
