        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-693",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "`scan_directory_path` \u2014 the function behind the primary CLI mode `picklescan -p <dir>` \u2014 gates which files get scanned on an extension allowlist that OMITS `_numpy_file_extensions`. The condition at scanner.py:604-606 is `if file_ext not in _zip_file_extensions and file_ext not in _pickle_file_extensions and file_ext not in _pytorch_file_extensions: continue`. Since `_numpy_file_extensions = {\".npy\"}` (line 217) is absent from that test, and `.npy` is in none of the three sets checked (`_zip_file_extensions = {\".zip\", \".npz\", \".7z\"}`, `_pickle_file_extensions = {\".pkl\", \".pickle\", \".joblib\", \".dat\", \".data\"}`, `_pytorch_file_extensions = {\".bin\", \".pt\", \".pth\", \".ckpt\"}`), every bare `.npy` file in a scanned directory tree is silently `continue`d and never scanned. This is an absence of coverage, not a dataflow bug: the capability to scan `.npy` exists and is wired up everywhere else \u2014 a dedicated `scan_numpy` method exists (line 482), `scan_bytes` explicitly dispatches `.npy` to it (`if file_ext is not None and file_ext in _numpy_file_extensions: return scan_numpy(...)`), and `scan_zip_bytes` scans `.npy` members found inside archives (line 471). Only the recursive directory walk omits it. A `.npy` holding an object-dtype array embeds a pickle that executes on `np.load(path, allow_pickle=True)`, so a repository containing a malicious `.npy` is reported with `Scanned files` not counting it and `issues_count == 0`, making the CLI exit 0 (\"clean\").\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: scan_directory_path (scanner.py:595-614) gates the os.walk purely on an extension allowlist of _zip_file_extensions / _pickle_file_extensions / _pytorch_file_extensions (lines 603-607) and `continue`s at 608 otherwise. `.npy` lives only in `_numpy_file_extensions = {\".npy\"}` (line 217) and is in none of the three tested sets, so every bare .npy in a scanned tree is skipped before open()/scan_bytes (611-612) and is not even counted in scanned_files. This is a coverage gap, not a missing feature: scan_numpy exists at 482 and routes object-dtype arrays to scan_pickle_bytes (510-511), scan_bytes dispatches .npy at 561-562, scan_zip_bytes scans .npy members at 471-474, and README.md:41 documents .npy support -- so a .npy inside a zip is scanned while a bare .npy sibling is not. Reachability is real, not just graph-asserted: cli.py:33 (-p/--path) -> cli.py:61-62 calls scan_directory_path for a directory, and cli.py:76 returns 0 when issues_count == 0, so a malicious .npy yields a \"clean\" exit 0. Graph agrees: lines 604/605/606 hold exactly three <operator>.notIn operands joined by one <operator>.logicalAnd with no _numpy_file_extensions term, and all calls in 600-612 plus CpgMethod scan_directory_path have reachable_from_entry=true. The gap is untested: the only .npy fixtures are in tests/data2/, while the sole walk test (tests/test_scanner.py:468-533) scans tests/data/, which has no .npy files. Corrections to the analyst: the condition spans 603-608, not 604-606, and the identical omission also exists in scan_huggingface_model (scanner.py:585-586), so `picklescan -hf` skips .npy too. Severity MEDIUM, not HIGH: it is a false-negative/detection bypass in a supply-chain gate (RCE lands downstream in a victim's np.load(allow_pickle=True)), and direct single-file scans via scan_file_path (617-622) are unaffected. Advisory version check: setup.cfg:3 pins first-party picklescan 1.0.0 and exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit-reference signal either way -- the verdict rests solely on the source and graph facts cited."
          },
