          "ruleId": "CWE-693",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "`scan_zip_bytes`'s content-sniffing fallback cannot recognise pickle protocol 0 or 1, so those payloads evade scanning entirely when the archive member's extension is not in `_pickle_file_extensions`. The gate at scanner.py:466 is `if file_ext in _pickle_file_extensions or any(magic_bytes.startswith(mn) for mn in _pickle_magic_bytes)`, and `_pickle_magic_bytes = {b\"\\x80\\x00\", b\"\\x80\\x01\", b\"\\x80\\x02\", b\"\\x80\\x03\", b\"\\x80\\x04\", b\"\\x80\\x05\"}` (line 223) only matches the PROTO opcode framing emitted by protocol 2+. Protocol 0/1 pickles carry no `\\x80` prefix \u2014 they begin directly with an opcode byte such as `c` (GLOBAL), `(` (MARK), `}` or `]`. Consequently a zip/pytorch archive member named e.g. `config.json`, `README.md`, `tokenizer.model` or `model.safetensors` whose body is a protocol-0 pickle satisfies neither branch and is skipped without even a warning. The repository's own test corpus proves protocol-0 payloads are live threats rather than theoretical: `tests/init_data_files.py` writes `data/malicious0.pkl` as raw bytes beginning `b'c__builtin__\\nglobals\\n...'` and `data/malicious10.pkl` as `b\"(S'raise RuntimeError(\\\"Injection running\\\")'\\ni__builtin__\\nexec\\n.\"` \u2014 both are unframed, both execute, and both would be invisible here. Note `scan_zip_bytes` is a reachable chokepoint (centrality 0.00119, reachable_from_entry = true) reached from every archive-bearing input path.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. scanner.py:466 gates whether a zip member gets scanned on `file_ext in _pickle_file_extensions or any(magic_bytes.startswith(mn) for mn in _pickle_magic_bytes)`, with no else branch and no warning \u2014 the only except (line 475) catches zipfile.BadZipFile/RuntimeError, so a non-matching member is silently skipped and scanned_files is not incremented, yielding a clean result. _pickle_magic_bytes (lines 223-230) contains only b\"\\x80\\x00\"..\"\\x80\\x05\"; the project's own comment at 221-222 concedes this matches only \"v2+\" PROTO framing. The PROTO opcode did not exist before protocol 2, so protocol-0/1 pickles begin directly with an opcode byte (`c`, `(`, `}`, `]`) and satisfy neither branch. The decisive proof is the repo's own test: init_data_files.py:780-784 builds malicious1_wrong_ext.zip with member \"data.txt\" (commented \"# Pickle file with a non-standard extension\") carrying pickle.dumps(Malicious1(), protocol=4), and test_scanner.py:259 asserts it IS detected as dangerous \u2014 detection depends entirely on the \\x80\\x04 sniff since .txt is not in the allowlist. Re-serializing that identical payload with protocol=0 (which the repo already does at line 729 for malicious3.pkl) defeats the only check. Protocol-0 payloads are in-scope threats per the project's own corpus (malicious0.pkl at 531-536 starts b'c__builtin__\\nglobals\\n...'; malicious10.pkl at 543-546 starts b\"(S'...\"), and scanner.py:102 (\"Pickle versions 0, 1, 2 have those function under '__builtin__'\") shows the scanner can flag them once it decides to scan. A realistic loader vector is documented in-repo: Malicious19 (init_data_files.py:119-127, 752) is partial(torch.load, path, pickle_file=\"config.json\", weights_only=False), i.e. torch.load pointed at an arbitrarily-named zip member. Reachability verified in the graph: scan_zip_bytes at line 452 reachable_from_entry=true, centrality 0.0011934, line-466 any/startswith calls reachable_from_entry=true, both call sites (scanner.py:523 in scan_pytorch, :567 in scan_bytes) reachable. Caveat: version is picklescan 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3) but exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no module/CVE version comparison was possible; this verdict rests solely on first-party source evidence and makes no published-CVE claim. Analyst's location 466 is correct; the defect properly spans the incomplete set at 223-230 as well."
