          "ruleId": "CWE-400",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "`_http_get` creates its connection with no socket timeout and reads the response body with no size ceiling. Line 256 is `conn = http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc)` \u2014 `http.client.HTTPSConnection` defaults to a global socket timeout that is normally `None` (block forever), and no `timeout=` keyword is supplied; line 267 is a bare `response.read()` with no `Content-Length` pre-check and no capped/chunked read. A malicious or merely unresponsive host reached through `picklescan -u <url>`/`-hf <model>` can therefore hang the scanner indefinitely, or return a multi-gigabyte body that is fully buffered into memory and then wrapped in `io.BytesIO` by `scan_url` (line 628) / `scan_huggingface_model` (line 589). Both the timeout control and the response-size control are absent repo-wide.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from the real source. `_http_get` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:250-269) builds its connection as `http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc)` with a single positional argument and no `timeout=` keyword (line 256), so the socket inherits `socket._GLOBAL_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT` -> `socket.getdefaulttimeout()` -> None, i.e. blocking forever on connect/send/recv. It then returns a bare `response.read()` (line 267) with no size argument, no Content-Length pre-check and no capped/chunked loop, so the entire body is buffered into one bytes object. Both controls are absent repo-wide: a grep across src/ for `timeout|setdefaulttimeout|Content-Length|content_length|max_size|MAX_` matched only the `_http_get` identifier itself (zero genuine timeout/size constructs), and `socket.setdefaulttimeout` appears nowhere in src/ (only `import socket` in tests/init_data_files.py, a test helper that cannot affect the installed CLI), so the global default really does stay None. Reachability is confirmed in source, not merely assumed: entry point `picklescan = picklescan.cli:main` (setup.cfg:30-32) -> cli.py:66 `scan_url(args.url)` / cli.py:68 `scan_huggingface_model(args.huggingface_model)` -> scanner.py:628 `scan_bytes(io.BytesIO(_http_get(url)), url)` and scanner.py:578/589 `_http_get(...)`; the graph independently marks lines 256 and 267 `reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1`. (The two cli.py call sites show `reachable_from_entry=false`, the known arrow/indirect under-linking artifact -- I verified the chain by reading the source, so this does not downgrade the lead.) Net effect: a malicious or merely unresponsive host reached via `picklescan -u <url>` or `-hf <model>` hangs the scanner indefinitely, or returns a multi-gigabyte body fully buffered in memory and then wrapped in io.BytesIO. One defect the analyst missed strengthens the same class: line 262 follows HTTP 302 by recursively calling `_http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])` with no redirect counter, opening a new HTTPS connection per hop, so an attacker-controlled redirect loop is a second exhaustion path in the same function. Exploit-reference check contributed nothing either way: the flaw is first-party code rather than a versioned dependency, and exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" -- so this verdict rests solely on the source evidence. Severity MEDIUM rather than HIGH: no code execution or disclosure and it needs the operator to target an attacker-controlled host, but that is picklescan's intended use (scanning untrusted models) and it is routinely run in automated CI malware-scanning pipelines, where an indefinite hang or OOM both wedges the job and can leave a model unscanned."
