          "ruleId": "CWE-693",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "`_list_globals` \u2014 the single chokepoint that enumerates every global the denylist later judges (highest centrality of any first-party method at 0.00270, reachable_from_entry = true) \u2014 handles no opcode that can push a callable via the copyreg extension registry. Its opcode dispatch is exhaustively: `MEMOIZE` (line 297), `PUT`/`BINPUT`/`LONG_BINPUT` (line 299), `GLOBAL`/`INST` (line 301), and `STACK_GLOBAL` (line 303). The `EXT1`, `EXT2` and `EXT4` opcodes are absent, as is any reference to `copyreg`. Those opcodes encode an integer extension code that the unpickler resolves through `copyreg._inverted_registry` into a `(module, name)` pair and pushes on the stack exactly as `GLOBAL` would, where a following `REDUCE` calls it. Because `_list_globals` never decodes them, such a global never enters the `globals` set, so `_build_scan_result_from_raw_globals` (line 368) has nothing to match against `_unsafe_globals` and contributes 0 to `issues_count` \u2014 the file is reported clean. Exploitability depends on the consuming process's registry contents, which the verifier should assess; the missing-control fact is that an entire class of global-loading opcode is unparsed.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. _list_globals (src/picklescan/scanner.py:272-365) has an exhaustive if/elif opcode dispatch covering only MEMOIZE (297), PUT/BINPUT/LONG_BINPUT (299), GLOBAL/INST (301) and STACK_GLOBAL (303); only the latter two populate the `globals` set consumed by _build_scan_result_from_raw_globals (368) and matched against _unsafe_globals (100-198). The EXT1/EXT2/EXT4 opcode family -- the remaining pickle opcodes that push a global onto the stack, resolved via copyreg's extension registry -- is unparsed: grep for EXT1|EXT2|EXT4|copyreg|_inverted_registry yields zero hits in src/ (only tests/init_data_files.py matches REDUCE). No secondary opcode guard exists: the other genops consumer, src/picklescan/torch.py:76-84, only extracts a magic number. The missing control is reachable: graph confirms reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0, centrality=0.0026995350361793315 for src/picklescan/scanner.py:<module>._list_globals at line 272. I additionally established corroboration the analyst did not cite: the clean verdict is robust to either genops behaviour, since if genops parsed the opcode raw_globals is empty (issues_count=0), and if genops errored with no globals found first, scan_pickle_bytes:417-420 returns ScanResult([], scanned_files=1, scan_err=False) -- clean with the error flag off. TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst: (1) their absence query conflates EXT with REDUCE/NEWOBJ/BUILD/OBJ, but those are NOT a gap -- picklescan by design reports dangerous imports, not calls, so only the EXT family is a genuine omission; (2) exploitability, which the analyst left unassessed, is weak -- copyreg's extension registry is empty by default in CPython (PEP 307 specified but no stdlib module populates it; copyreg.add_extension() must be called explicitly by the application), and nothing in the scanned file can populate it. On a default interpreter an EXT-bearing pickle scans clean but is also inert at load time (unregistered extension code -> load-time error), so this is a false negative on a harmless file. Code execution would additionally require the consuming process to have pre-registered a dangerous callable, which is not attacker-controlled via the file and would itself be the primary defect. Hence a genuine, reachable detection-robustness gap in a security scanner, but not a usable bypass -- severity LOW, not the MEDIUM implied. Version pinned to picklescan 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit-reference signal in either direction is claimed."
