          "ruleId": "CWE-674",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "No recursion-depth or expansion limit bounds nested-archive scanning, giving an unbounded self-recursive cycle through disk. `scan_7z_bytes` selects members ending in a `_pickle_file_extensions` suffix, extracts them, and calls `scan_file_path(file_path)` (line 445-446); `scan_file_path` calls `scan_bytes(file, path, file_ext)` (line 622); and `scan_bytes` sniffs content and re-dispatches with `elif _is_7z_file(data): return scan_7z_bytes(data, file_id)` and `if is_zip: return scan_zip_bytes(data, file_id)`. A 7z archive containing a member literally named `payload.pkl` whose bytes are themselves a 7z archive containing `payload.pkl`, repeated, therefore recurses `scan_7z_bytes -> scan_file_path -> scan_bytes -> scan_7z_bytes` indefinitely, each level writing a fresh `TemporaryDirectory` to disk (line 441) and consuming a stack frame. None of the scan functions carries a depth or budget parameter \u2014 the signatures are `scan_7z_bytes(data, file_id)`, `scan_zip_bytes(data, file_id)`, `scan_bytes(data, file_id, file_ext=None)`, `scan_file_path(path)` \u2014 and no size, ratio, member-count, or depth ceiling exists anywhere in `src/picklescan/`, so a small crafted input can exhaust stack, disk, and time in the scanner that CI pipelines run on untrusted models.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently verified: scan_7z_bytes selects archive members by filename suffix alone (scanner.py:439, _pickle_file_extensions at :219), extracts them into a nested TemporaryDirectory (:441-442), and calls scan_file_path (:447); scan_file_path forwards to scan_bytes (:622); scan_bytes content-sniffs and re-dispatches `elif _is_7z_file(data): return scan_7z_bytes(...)` (:568-569), closing a self-recursive cycle. A Cypher query over CONTAINS_CALL reproduced exactly that 3-edge cycle (scan_7z_bytes@428 -447-> scan_file_path@617 -622-> scan_bytes@548 -569-> scan_7z_bytes@428), all nodes reachable_from_entry=true, entered directly from cli.py:64 (--path) and cli.py:66/scanner.py:628 (--url). Grep of all of src/picklescan/ for depth|recursion|setrecursionlimit|max_|limit found no depth counter, member-count cap, size ceiling, or decompression-ratio check, and none of the four scan signatures carries a budget parameter. Path is live code: tests/init_data_files.py:757-759 builds a 7z whose member is named data.pkl, scanned at tests/test_scanner.py:258, so an attacker only swaps that member's bytes for another 7z. Two corrections to the analyst: (1) the zip leg is NOT part of the cycle -- scan_zip_bytes (:452-479) only calls scan_pickle_bytes/scan_numpy and never re-dispatches, so zip-in-zip does not recurse; the recursion is 7z-only. (2) The recursion call is at line 447, not 445-446. Pure stack depth is partially mitigated (~4 frames/level vs the 1000-frame default, and cli.py:77 catches Exception so RecursionError yields exit 2), but the unbounded impact is breadth x amplification: unlimited .pkl members per level each being a nested archive gives N^D extractions, all levels' temp dirs coexist on disk because the `with TemporaryDirectory()` blocks nest, and archive.extract writes the full LZMA-decompressed size with no ratio cap (CWE-409 co-present). Severity MEDIUM: availability-only impact, no code execution, but it is triggered by exactly the untrusted-model input this scanner exists to process in CI. py7zr is unpinned (setup.cfg:25), so no dependency version could be pinned; the defect is in picklescan 1.0.0's own control flow, and the exploit corpus returned \"unavailable: not indexed\", which does not bear on the verdict."
