          "ruleId": "CWE-697",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Type confusion inside the _unsafe_globals literal: three entries hold a BARE STRING instead of a set \u2014 `tmp1[\"functools\"] = \"partial\"`, `tmp1[\"torch.serialization\"] = \"load\"`, `tmp1[\"torch._inductor.codecache\"] = \"compile_file\"` \u2014 whereas every other non-wildcard entry is a set (e.g. `{\"FileIO\"}`, `{\"CodeType\"}`, `{\"runctx\",\"run\"}`). The classification test at line 393 is `unsafe_filter is not None and (unsafe_filter == \"*\" or g.name in unsafe_filter)`. When unsafe_filter is a str, `g.name in unsafe_filter` is Python SUBSTRING containment rather than set membership, so every substring of the intended name also matches: `functools.part`, `functools.ial`, `functools.a`, `torch.serialization.loa`, `torch.serialization.ad`, and even the empty name are all reported SafetyLevel.Dangerous. The intended names still match (a string contains itself \u2014 the repo's tests pass, asserting Dangerous for `functools partial`, `torch.serialization load`, `torch._inductor.codecache compile_file`), so the impact direction is FALSE POSITIVES, not a bypass: an attacker who wants a benign model to be reported as malicious can embed a harmless `functools` attribute whose name is a substring of \"partial\", poisoning CI gates that fail on issues_count > 0. Line 393 is the single decision chokepoint every scanned global passes through, so the blast radius covers all scan entry points.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. Three entries in the _unsafe_globals literal hold a bare str instead of a set: src/picklescan/scanner.py:136 `\"functools\": \"partial\"`, :182 `\"torch._inductor.codecache\": \"compile_file\"`, :185 `\"torch.serialization\": \"load\"` \u2014 every other non-\"*\" entry is a set (L130 {\"CodeType\"}, L138 {\"FileIO\"}, L162 {\"runctx\",\"run\"}). L378 assigns `unsafe_filter = _unsafe_globals.get(g.module)` verbatim, so at the single classification chokepoint L393 (`unsafe_filter is not None and (unsafe_filter == \"*\" or g.name in unsafe_filter)`) the `in` operator performs Python SUBSTRING containment rather than set membership for those three modules. g.name is fully attacker-controlled: L376 builds Global from rg[1], produced by _list_globals at L302 `globals.add(tuple(op_value.split(\" \", 1)))` on the raw GLOBAL/INST opcode argument. A crafted pickle with `GLOBAL \"functools art\"` therefore makes `\"art\" in \"partial\"` true and is reported SafetyLevel.Dangerous with issues_count incremented. Reachability verified both ways: the graph marks L393's `<operator>.in` node reachable_from_entry=true and _build_scan_result_from_raw_globals is an :EntryPoint with exposure=reachable; in source it is called at L416/L424 from scan_pickle_bytes, reached via scan_bytes (L548), scan_file_path (L617), scan_url, scan_huggingface_model and cli.main. I also verified the impact DIRECTION matches the lead: substring containment is strictly more permissive than set membership and a string contains itself, so the intended names still match \u2014 tests/test_scanner.py:516,518,519 assert Dangerous for exactly those three and pass, making the bug latent. I checked the reverse (bypass) direction and found none: a longer name such as `_legacy_load` fails both `in \"load\"` and `in {\"load\"}`, so no detection is lost. The sibling safe path is clean: _safe_globals (L66-98) is all set literals, so L397 `g.name in safe_filter` is unaffected. I downgrade severity from the analyst's MEDIUM confidence to LOW: the defect fails CLOSED (over-flagging only, never a scanner bypass), and no genuine attribute of functools or torch.serialization is a proper substring of \"partial\"/\"load\" (reduce, wraps, partialmethod, save all fail), so real models produce no accidental false positives \u2014 the only achievable effect is an attacker making their own artifact look malicious to poison a CI gate that fails on issues_count > 0. Advisory exploit check not applicable: this is first-party code with no pinned version, and the Metasploit corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"."
