xtensions = {\".bin\",\".pt\",\".pth\",\".ckpt\"} (218) and _numpy_file_extensions = {\".npy\"} (217) are defined but not consulted by the 7z filter \u2014 the exact extensions picklescan treats as pickles at top level are silently skipped inside a 7z. Reachability verified in real source, not only the graph: scan_file_path (617) -> scan_bytes (548) -> _is_7z_file -> scan_7z_bytes (568-569), and scan_pytorch (524-525) routes a .pt/.bin artifact that is actually a 7z container into the same filter; scan_url, scan_directory_path and scan_huggingface_model all funnel through scan_bytes, and _zip_file_extensions (220) includes \".7z\" so the directory/HF pre-filters do not drop it. Graph concurs (scan_7z_bytes line 428, reachable_from_entry=true, exposure=\"reachable\"). Test coverage explains why it was missed: the sole 7z fixture uses a .pkl member only (tests/init_data_files.py:757-760, exercised at tests/test_scanner.py:258). This is a protection-mechanism failure / detection bypass in a security scanner: a trusted \"clean\" verdict on an archive that contains an unscanned malicious pickle leads to downstream arbitrary code execution when the victim extracts and loads it. Exploit-reference step was advisory only and yielded nothing \u2014 version pinned at picklescan 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3) but the Metasploit corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no module/CVE version comparison was possible; I assert neither patched nor weaponized status from it. The confirmation rests entirely on the first-party source evidence above."
