          "message": {
            "text": "The literal _pickle_magic_bytes = {b\"\\x80\\x00\", b\"\\x80\\x01\", b\"\\x80\\x02\", b\"\\x80\\x03\", b\"\\x80\\x04\", b\"\\x80\\x05\"} (line 223) only enumerates the pickle PROTO opcode (0x80), which is emitted exclusively by pickle protocols 2-5. Pickle protocols 0 and 1 contain NO PROTO opcode and begin with an ASCII/raw opcode instead (e.g. `c` = GLOBAL, `(` = MARK, `]` = EMPTY_LIST). scan_zip_bytes scans a zip/npz member only if `file_ext in _pickle_file_extensions or any(magic_bytes.startswith(mn) for mn in _pickle_magic_bytes)`. Therefore a malicious PROTOCOL-0 or PROTOCOL-1 pickle placed in a .zip/.npz under an extension outside _pickle_file_extensions (e.g. `data.bin`, `model.pt`, `notes.txt`, no extension) matches neither branch and is silently skipped \u2014 never passed to scan_pickle_bytes. The scanner's own parser handles these protocols fine (_list_globals explicitly dispatches on the proto-0/1 `GLOBAL` and `INST` opcodes), so the gap is purely in the magic-byte literal, not the parser. Corroborating: the entry `b\"\\x80\\x00\"` is dead weight (pickle never emits PROTO 0), showing the set was authored as \"the PROTO opcode\" and protocol 0/1 framing was simply not considered.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently and the lead holds on every load-bearing point. (1) src/picklescan/scanner.py:223-230 defines _pickle_magic_bytes as only {\\x80\\x00..\\x80\\x05}, i.e. the PROTO opcode, and the code's own comment at line 221-222 admits the limitation verbatim: \"Pickle files do not actually have magic bytes, but v2+ files start with a PROTO (\\x80) opcode\". CPython's Pickler.dump emits PROTO only for proto >= 2, so protocol-0/1 streams begin with a bare opcode (c GLOBAL, ( MARK, ] EMPTY_LIST) and never with \\x80. (2) The zip-member gate at scanner.py:466 is `if file_ext in _pickle_file_extensions or any(magic_bytes.startswith(mn) for mn in _pickle_magic_bytes)` with an elif for numpy at 471 and NO else clause (the only trailing clause, 475-477, is `except (zipfile.BadZipFile, RuntimeError)`), so an unmatched member is silently dropped and not even counted in scanned_files. _pickle_file_extensions (line 219) is {.pkl,.pickle,.joblib,.dat,.data}, excluding .txt/.bin/.pt/no-extension. (3) The gap is in the literal, not the parser: _list_globals dispatches proto-0/1 opcodes at scanner.py:301-302 (`elif op_name in (\"GLOBAL\",\"INST\")`), and the project deliberately tests protocol 0 (tests/init_data_files.py:522-525 \"Test with Pickle versions 0, 3, and 4\"; test_scanner.py:245 asserts malicious1_v0.pkl is detected). (4) Decisive control/variant pair from the project's own suite: tests/init_data_files.py:780-784 builds malicious1_wrong_ext.zip with member \"data.txt\" (\"# Pickle file with a non-standard extension\") at protocol=4, and tests/test_scanner.py:259 asserts it IS reported malicious - meaning the magic-byte branch is the sole defense against extension renaming inside archives. Changing only that protocol=4 to protocol=0 makes the identical payload match neither branch at line 466, yielding a clean verdict. SECURITY.md:5 names false negatives as reportable vulnerabilities, so this is not an accepted limitation. (5) Reachability confirmed in source (CLI -> scan_file_path:617 -> scan_bytes:548 -> scan_zip_bytes:567, plus scan_pytorch:523) and in the graph (line 466 logicalOr/any/startswith all reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1; both scan_zip_bytes call sites at 523 and 567 reachable_from_entry=true). Version pinned at picklescan 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" so no exploit-reference evidence either way was used. Correcting the analyst's location: line 223 is the root-cause literal but the defect manifests at the gate, 466-477. Severity MEDIUM rather than HIGH because exploitation requires a downstream loader to actually consume the smuggled member (torch.load/np.load read fixed member names), so the clean path is the generic-.zip / unzip-then-pickle.load case rather than universal RCE."
