          "ruleId": "CWE-184",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The _unsafe_globals denylist literal (line 100) maps `\"builtins\"` and `\"__builtin__\"` to the set {\"eval\",\"compile\",\"getattr\",\"apply\",\"exec\",\"open\",\"breakpoint\"} \u2014 which OMITS `__import__`, the canonical pickle dynamic-import gadget. Because `getattr` IS present (clearly added to break the `getattr(__import__(\"os\"),\"system\")` chain), the omission of `__import__` itself is an inconsistency in the literal rather than a deliberate allowance. Consequence: a global `builtins __import__` is classified only SafetyLevel.Suspicious, which does NOT increment issues_count in _build_scan_result_from_raw_globals and therefore does not mark the file infected or drive a non-zero CLI exit. The repo's own test suite encodes this gap as expected behaviour, asserting `Global(\"builtins\", \"__import__\", SafetyLevel.Suspicious)` for the GHSA-955r-x9j8-7rhh and GHSA-46h3-79wf-xr6c payloads \u2014 those files are only caught incidentally via the accompanying `_operator.methodcaller`/`attrgetter`, so a gadget chain reaching `__import__` through a non-denylisted caller would not be flagged. Related omissions visible in the same literal and the same tests: `builtins.globals` and `_codecs.encode` are also only Suspicious, and `importlib` has no key at all even though the semantically equivalent `pydoc` is denylisted \"*\".\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Every element of the lead is verified in the real source. In src/picklescan/scanner.py the _unsafe_globals literal maps both \"builtins\" (111-120) and \"__builtin__\" (101-110) to {\"eval\",\"compile\",\"getattr\",\"apply\",\"exec\",\"open\",\"breakpoint\"}; `getattr` is present but `__import__` is absent, and `importlib` has no key while the equivalent `pydoc` is \"*\". In _build_scan_result_from_raw_globals (368-403), only the unsafe/dangerous branch increments issues_count (line 396); a module that matches an entry but whose name is not in the set falls to the else at line 400 -> SafetyLevel.Suspicious with no increment (confirmed by both read and the graph query). issues_count drives infected_files (line 403) and the CLI exit code (cli.py:76), so `builtins __import__` is classified only Suspicious and cannot by itself mark a file infected or yield a non-zero exit. The repo's tests enshrine this: test_scanner.py:392 and :396 assert Global(\"builtins\",\"__import__\",SafetyLevel.Suspicious) for GHSA-955r-x9j8-7rhh and GHSA-46h3-79wf-xr6c (those files are flagged only incidentally via the accompanying Dangerous _operator.methodcaller/attrgetter), and :490/:506/:509 confirm __builtin__ globals, builtins __import__, and _codecs encode are all only Suspicious. This is a real incomplete-denylist detection gap (CWE-184) in a security scanner."
